window.CONTRACTOR_BLOQUE_8={number:8,title:"Seguridad y OSHA",tagline:"Una muerte en el job = tu empresa desaparece",intro:`
    <p><strong>Cal/OSHA deber\xEDa asustarte m\xE1s que el CSLB.</strong> Si el CSLB te suspende la licencia, puedes volver a abrir bajo otra entidad y recuperarte en 6-12 meses. Si Cal/OSHA te pega una <strong>willful serious citation</strong> despu\xE9s de una muerte en el job, est\xE1s viendo multas de <strong>$158,727 por violaci\xF3n</strong>, un Workers' Comp X-Mod de 2.0+ que te vuelve no-asegurable, y posible <strong>criminal liability</strong> bajo Penal Code 192(b) (involuntary manslaughter) para ti como owner.</p>
    <p>California no es OSHA federal \u2014 Cal/OSHA es <strong>m\xE1s estricto</strong>. Tiene sus propios standards (Title 8 California Code of Regulations, conocido como "8 CCR"), sus propios inspectores (DOSH \u2014 Division of Occupational Safety and Health), y multas m\xE1s altas que federal. Los HVAC contractors est\xE1n en el top 5 de industrias m\xE1s citadas en California, junto con construction general, roofing, tree care, y landscaping.</p>
    <p>Este bloque cubre lo que cada owner-operator y foreman HVAC necesita saber: c\xF3mo escribir tu <strong>IIPP</strong> (Injury & Illness Prevention Program) obligatorio bajo <strong>8 CCR 3203</strong>, c\xF3mo correr <strong>JSA</strong> (Job Safety Analysis) y toolbox talks semanales con sign-in roster, los 10 hazards que matan HVAC techs (electrical, ladder falls, heat stress, confined space en \xE1ticos, refrigerant burns), <strong>PPE</strong> obligatorio con standards ANSI espec\xEDficos, <strong>LOTO</strong> (Lockout/Tagout) bajo 8 CCR 3314, el Heat Illness Prevention Standard 8 CCR 3395 (uno de los m\xE1s agresivos del pa\xEDs), c\xF3mo llenar los OSHA 300/300A/301 logs, cu\xE1ndo hacer el <strong>Serious Injury Report</strong> de 8 horas, y c\xF3mo pelear una citation sin perder tu licencia.</p>
    <p><strong>Regla #1:</strong> Un tech muerto en el job es el fin de tu empresa. No hay seguro que cubra tu negligence cuando no tuviste IIPP, no hiciste toolbox talks, o no entrenaste LOTO. Los plaintiff attorneys viven de contratistas sin IIPP.</p>
    <p><strong>Regla #2:</strong> La visita de Cal/OSHA no es "si" \u2014 es "cu\xE1ndo." Un ex-empleado enojado, un vecino que ve a tus techs sin hard hat en un roof, un accidente reportado por el ER\u2026 cualquier cosa dispara una inspecci\xF3n. Y cuando llegan, tienes que entregar: IIPP escrito, toolbox talk rosters, training records, Cal/OSHA 300 logs de los \xFAltimos 5 a\xF1os, y tu SDS binder. Si no tienes eso, cada d\xEDa sin = una citation por d\xEDa.</p>
  `,sections:[{id:"cal-osha-basics",heading:"Cal/OSHA vs OSHA Federal \u2014 California es M\xE1s Estricto",body:`
        <p>Muchos contratistas llegan de Texas, Florida o Arizona y asumen que las reglas federales de OSHA aplican igual en California. <strong>Error costoso.</strong> California tiene su propio programa aprobado por OSHA federal desde 1973 (uno de 22 "state plan" states), y en cada categor\xEDa Cal/OSHA es igual o m\xE1s estricto que federal.</p>

        <h4>Las diferencias clave que te van a pegar</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>IIPP obligatorio para TODOS los employers</strong> (8 CCR 3203) \u2014 federal OSHA no lo requiere. En CA, si tienes aunque sea 1 empleado, necesitas IIPP escrito. Sin IIPP = citation autom\xE1tica Serious $25,000.</li>
          <li><strong>Heat Illness Prevention</strong> (8 CCR 3395) \u2014 CA tiene el standard m\xE1s agresivo del pa\xEDs. Federal OSHA est\xE1 apenas proponiendo un standard similar. En CA desde 2005 outdoor y desde 2024 indoor.</li>
          <li><strong>Fall protection a 6 ft</strong> en residential construction (8 CCR 1670) \u2014 federal permite 6 ft tambi\xE9n pero CA es m\xE1s estricto en rooftop work y anchorage requirements.</li>
          <li><strong>Multas m\xE1s altas:</strong> Cal/OSHA maximums (2025) \u2014 Serious $25,000, Willful/Repeat $158,727. Federal OSHA es $16,131 Serious y $161,323 Willful, pero Cal/OSHA emite M\xC1S citations per capita.</li>
          <li><strong>Reporting timeline m\xE1s corto</strong> en algunos casos \u2014 serious injury 8 horas para Cal/OSHA (vs federal tambi\xE9n 8 hrs, pero CA cuenta hospitalization overnight mientras federal requiere inpatient).</li>
          <li><strong>8 CCR</strong> (California Code of Regulations, Title 8) = la biblia. Federal OSHA usa 29 CFR 1910 (General Industry) y 29 CFR 1926 (Construction). En CA usas 8 CCR Division 1 (General Industry Safety Orders) y Division 4 (Construction Safety Orders).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Posting requirements \u2014 lo que DEBE colgar en tu shop y job trailer</h4>
        <p>Cal/OSHA inspectors abren por el poster wall. Si no tienes estos posters visibles, cada uno es una citation separada.</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cal/OSHA Safety and Health Protection on the Job poster</strong> (DWC 7) \u2014 gratis en dir.ca.gov, debe estar en ingl\xE9s y en el idioma predominante de tus empleados (espa\xF1ol si 10%+ son hispanos).</li>
          <li><strong>Workers' Compensation Notice to Employees</strong> (DWC 7) \u2014 nombre del carrier, policy number, claims adjuster contact, hospital pre-designation form.</li>
          <li><strong>IIPP Summary</strong> \u2014 resumen del Injury & Illness Prevention Program con nombre del safety coordinator.</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency Phone Numbers</strong> \u2014 911, nearest hospital, poison control (1-800-222-1222), Cal/OSHA local office.</li>
          <li><strong>Cal/OSHA 300A Annual Summary</strong> \u2014 posted from Feb 1 to Apr 30 cada a\xF1o con el total de injuries del a\xF1o anterior (incluso si fue cero).</li>
          <li><strong>California Minimum Wage poster</strong>, Paid Sick Leave, Pregnancy Disability, Sexual Harassment \u2014 varios m\xE1s del DIR (Department of Industrial Relations).</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency Action Plan (EAP)</strong> y ruta de evacuaci\xF3n visible en shop y en cada trailer.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Registro con Cal/OSHA \u2014 DOSH y Contractor Registration</h4>
        <p>Adem\xE1s del CSLB, tienes que registrarte con <strong>DIR</strong> (Department of Industrial Relations) si haces trabajo p\xFAblico \u2014 CRS (Contractor Registration System) en dir.ca.gov, renewal anual $400. Los contractors de HVAC residential privado no necesitan DIR registration, pero s\xED Workers' Comp certificate on file con CSLB.</p>

        <h4>Tu supervisor \u2014 el "designated competent person"</h4>
        <p>Cal/OSHA requiere que tengas un <strong>Competent Person</strong> designado para ciertas actividades (fall protection, scaffolding, trenching, LOTO). Para HVAC t\xEDpicamente es el foreman o lead tech. Debe tener training documentado y autoridad para parar el trabajo si ve un hazard. Sin competent person documentado = Serious citation $25K.</p>
      `,keyPoints:["Cal/OSHA es un state plan aprobado \u2014 m\xE1s estricto que federal en casi todo","IIPP escrito es OBLIGATORIO con 1+ empleado (8 CCR 3203)","8 CCR = California Code of Regulations Title 8, tu biblia","Posters obligatorios: Cal/OSHA, Workers Comp, IIPP Summary, 300A","Competent Person designado por actividad (fall, LOTO, scaffold)","Multas 2025: Serious $25K, Willful/Repeat $158,727 por violaci\xF3n"],realTalk:'"Vi un contratista perder 11 a\xF1os de negocio en 6 meses. Un tech se cay\xF3 de un ladder en Fresno \u2014 no era fatal pero fue hospitalized. No report\xF3 a Cal/OSHA en 8 hrs (no sab\xEDa que ten\xEDa que hacerlo), no ten\xEDa IIPP escrito, no ten\xEDa toolbox talks documented. Total de citations: $287,000. Perdi\xF3 el CSLB license por workers comp fraud porque reclasific\xF3 al tech como \u2018independent contractor\u2019 despu\xE9s del accidente. No pudo pagar, cerr\xF3. Todo empez\xF3 porque no ten\xEDa 3 hojas de IIPP."',checklist:["IIPP escrito en ingl\xE9s y espa\xF1ol (8 CCR 3203)","Safety coordinator designado por nombre","Cal/OSHA poster colgado en shop y trailer","Workers Comp Notice con carrier info visible","OSHA 300A posted Feb 1 - Apr 30","EAP (Emergency Action Plan) visible","Competent person designado para fall/LOTO/scaffold","DIR registration si haces trabajo p\xFAblico (prevailing wage)"],commonMistakes:["Usar IIPP template gen\xE9rico \u2014 Cal/OSHA lo rechaza si no menciona hazards espec\xEDficos de HVAC","Posters solo en ingl\xE9s cuando 10%+ de empleados hablan espa\xF1ol","No renovar DIR registration \u2014 $400/a\xF1o, multa si lapso","Asumir que federal OSHA aplica \u2014 CA tiene sus propios standards","No designar competent person documentado por escrito","Olvidar postear 300A en Feb-Abril \u2014 citation por cada d\xEDa tarde"]},{id:"jsa-toolbox-talks",heading:"JSA Diario y Toolbox Talks Semanales",body:`
        <p>La diferencia entre una empresa HVAC que sobrevive una inspecci\xF3n de Cal/OSHA y una que colapsa est\xE1 en dos documentos: el <strong>JSA</strong> (Job Safety Analysis) diario y el <strong>Toolbox Talk</strong> semanal con sign-in roster. Si no tienes estos dos, no tienes un safety program \u2014 tienes un cementerio de excusas.</p>

        <h4>JSA \u2014 Job Safety Analysis (tambi\xE9n llamado AHA o Pre-Task Plan)</h4>
        <p>Antes de empezar trabajo en CADA job site nuevo, el foreman llena un JSA de 1-2 p\xE1ginas identificando:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Task breakdown</strong> \u2014 cada paso del trabajo (ej: "1. Descargar equipo. 2. Subir condensador al roof. 3. Recuperar refrigerante R-410A del sistema existente. 4. Soldar l\xEDneas. 5. Pressure test. 6. Pull vacuum. 7. Start-up.")</li>
          <li><strong>Hazards por paso</strong> \u2014 qu\xE9 puede salir mal en cada fase (ej: "Paso 3: exposici\xF3n a R-410A, frostbite en manos, possible ruptura de l\xEDnea alta presi\xF3n.")</li>
          <li><strong>Controls</strong> \u2014 c\xF3mo mitigas cada hazard (ej: "PPE: gloves de nitrilo + face shield. Ventilaci\xF3n del \xE1rea. Recovery machine inspected. No recovery en \xE1reas confined sin ventilaci\xF3n mec\xE1nica.")</li>
          <li><strong>PPE requerido</strong> para ese job espec\xEDfico</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency info</strong> \u2014 hospital m\xE1s cercano, nearest eye wash, 911 directions al job site</li>
          <li><strong>Sign-off</strong> \u2014 cada tech firma que ley\xF3 y entendi\xF3 el JSA ANTES de empezar</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Tiempo real:</strong> 10-15 minutos al arranque del d\xEDa. Se archiva por 5 a\xF1os. Cal/OSHA lo pide durante inspection y es la diferencia entre Serious citation $25K y General citation $15K (o ninguna).</p>

        <h4>Toolbox Talk Semanal \u2014 Sign-in Roster Obligatorio</h4>
        <p>Una vez por semana (Cal/OSHA no lo manda textualmente, pero el IIPP lo requiere como "frequent inspections" bajo 3203(a)(4)), el foreman junta a todos los techs para un safety meeting de 15-20 minutos sobre un tema espec\xEDfico.</p>
        <p><strong>Temas rotativos recomendados para HVAC (52 semanas):</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Ladder safety \u2014 3 points of contact, angle 4:1, extension 3 ft above roof edge</li>
          <li>Electrical safety \u2014 LOTO, 30mA GFCI, NFPA 70E arc flash PPE</li>
          <li>Heat illness prevention \u2014 acclimatization, water/shade/rest, high-heat trigger 95\xB0F</li>
          <li>Refrigerant handling \u2014 R-410A frostbite, asphyxiation en confined, EPA 608</li>
          <li>Fall protection \u2014 6 ft trigger, anchor points 5000 lbs, harness inspection pre-use</li>
          <li>Hand tool safety \u2014 guards, inspection, proper gloves</li>
          <li>Hearing protection \u2014 85 dBA action level, duct blower = 95-105 dBA</li>
          <li>Hazcom/GHS \u2014 SDS binder, label reading, pictogram meanings</li>
          <li>Confined space en \xE1ticos \u2014 oxygen deficiency, extreme heat 130\xB0F+</li>
          <li>Driving safety \u2014 service vans, distracted driving, ladder tie-down</li>
          <li>Respiratory protection \u2014 R-410A OEL, fiberglass insulation dust, mold in condensate pans</li>
          <li>PPE inspection \u2014 hard hat expiration, safety glasses ANSI Z87.1, boot dielectric rating</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Sign-in Roster \u2014 Lo que debe capturar</h4>
        <ul>
          <li>Fecha, hora de inicio y fin</li>
          <li>Topic del meeting (ej: "Ladder Safety \u2014 3 Points of Contact")</li>
          <li>Nombre del presenter (foreman)</li>
          <li>Lista de asistentes con firma (no inicial \u2014 firma completa)</li>
          <li>Employee ID o last 4 del SSN</li>
          <li>Comentarios/preguntas del team</li>
          <li>Acknowledgment statement: "I attended this meeting and understand the information presented."</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Top 10 Hazards que matan HVAC Techs en California</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Electrocution</strong> \u2014 trabajar en live circuits, no LOTO, capacitor discharge \u2014 top killer HVAC</li>
          <li><strong>Falls from ladders/roofs</strong> \u2014 extension ladder not secured, walking on pitched roofs sin harness</li>
          <li><strong>Heat stroke</strong> \u2014 attics 130-160\xB0F en verano CA, Central Valley</li>
          <li><strong>Confined space asphyxiation</strong> \u2014 \xE1ticos sin ventilation, refrigerant leaks displacing oxygen</li>
          <li><strong>Refrigerant burns</strong> \u2014 R-410A frostbite, hot gas burns en compressor discharge</li>
          <li><strong>Vehicle accidents</strong> \u2014 service van crashes, ladder/ejection impalement</li>
          <li><strong>Struck-by</strong> \u2014 equipment lift failure, condensadores cayendo del roof</li>
          <li><strong>Caught-in/between</strong> \u2014 fan blades, belt drives, compressor disassembly</li>
          <li><strong>Fire/explosion</strong> \u2014 torch work near flammables, propane leaks, natural gas</li>
          <li><strong>Chemical exposure</strong> \u2014 acid wash coils (hydrofluoric/muriatic), ammonia in commercial, refrigerant decomposition products</li>
        </ol>
      `,keyPoints:["JSA diario es la diferencia entre Serious $25K y General $15K","Toolbox talks semanales con sign-in roster (obligatorio por IIPP)","Guarda JSA y rosters por 5 a\xF1os m\xEDnimo","Top killers HVAC: electrocution, falls, heat, confined space","52 topics de safety \u2014 rotar a\xF1o completo sin repetir","Sign-in debe tener firma completa, no inicial"],realTalk:'"El JSA diario parece burocracia hasta el d\xEDa que un tech se quema con R-410A y el plaintiff attorney te pregunta \u2018\xBFqu\xE9 hazards identific\xF3 su company para este job?\u2019 Si no tienes el JSA, pagas el settlement completo. Si lo tienes con la firma del tech, el caso se va por comparative negligence y pagas 40%. Diferencia: $800K vs $2M."',checklist:["JSA template pre-printed para cada tipo de job (install, service, duct, ventilation)","Foreman llena JSA antes de que nadie toque tools","Todos los techs firman JSA antes de trabajar","Toolbox talk roster cada lunes con topic del d\xEDa","Archivo f\xEDsico + digital (HouseCall Pro, ServiceTitan) por 5 a\xF1os","Rotar topics por 52 semanas sin repetir","Documentar incidents y near-misses para pr\xF3ximo toolbox"],commonMistakes:["Firmar todos los JSA de la semana el viernes \u2014 fraud, Cal/OSHA lo detecta por tinta/pluma","Usar mismo JSA gen\xE9rico para cada job \u2014 in\xFAtil, no identifica hazards reales","Toolbox talks solo en ingl\xE9s cuando 80% del team es hispano \u2014 citation",'No incluir sign-in roster \u2014 el meeting "no existi\xF3" legalmente',"Olvidar topics de confined space attics \u2014 matan techs en verano","No rotar topics \u2014 repetir ladder safety 20 veces es in\xFAtil"]},{id:"ppe-requirements",heading:"PPE Requerido \u2014 Standards ANSI Espec\xEDficos",body:`
        <p>PPE (Personal Protective Equipment) es el \xFAltimo layer de defensa despu\xE9s de engineering controls y administrative controls. Cal/OSHA bajo <strong>8 CCR 3380-3400</strong> requiere PPE cuando el hazard no se puede eliminar. Para HVAC cada pieza tiene un standard ANSI espec\xEDfico \u2014 si compras el wrong rating, la citation dice "PPE inadecuado" y equivale a no-PPE.</p>

        <h4>Hard hat \u2014 ANSI Z89.1 Type I Class E</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulaci\xF3n:</strong> 8 CCR 1514 (construction head protection)</li>
          <li><strong>Standard:</strong> ANSI Z89.1 \u2014 Type I (top impact) Class E (electrical, 20,000V resist)</li>
          <li><strong>HVAC tip:</strong> Class E es cr\xEDtico porque trabajas cerca de service panels. Class G (2,200V) no es suficiente. Class C (conductive aluminum) prohibido en HVAC.</li>
          <li><strong>Expiration:</strong> Shell 5 a\xF1os desde manufacture, suspension 1 a\xF1o desde primer uso. Fecha impresa en interior.</li>
          <li><strong>Cu\xE1ndo es mandatory:</strong> Cualquier overhead hazard \u2014 equipment lifts, rooftop work con crane, trenching para ductwork underground.</li>
          <li><strong>Sticker allowed:</strong> Peque\xF1os stickers ok en hard hat moderno (2018+ Type I). Paint NO \u2014 degrada el plastic.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Safety glasses \u2014 ANSI Z87.1+</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulaci\xF3n:</strong> 8 CCR 3382</li>
          <li><strong>Standard:</strong> ANSI Z87.1-2020 marking "Z87+" en frame (impact-rated). Solo "Z87" sin "+" es dress eyewear, no construction.</li>
          <li><strong>Side shields:</strong> Mandatory para HVAC cuando trabajas en service panels, soldering, grinding.</li>
          <li><strong>Face shield:</strong> Requerido ENCIMA de safety glasses (no en lugar de) para acid wash, refrigerant charging, arc flash.</li>
          <li><strong>Prescription:</strong> Over-the-glasses (OTG) model o prescription Z87+ \u2014 empleador paga si safety glasses son requeridos (Labor Code 2802).</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Cut-resistant gloves \u2014 ANSI/ISEA 105 level A4+</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulaci\xF3n:</strong> 8 CCR 3384</li>
          <li><strong>Standard:</strong> ANSI/ISEA 105-2016 \u2014 levels A1 (lowest) to A9 (highest)</li>
          <li><strong>HVAC minimum:</strong> A4 para sheet metal handling, duct work, refrigerant line unpacking</li>
          <li><strong>Dielectric gloves:</strong> ASTM D120 Class 00 (500V AC) para low-voltage HVAC, Class 0 (1,000V) para residential service, Class 1 (7,500V) si tocas commercial 480V. Leather protector OBLIGATORIO sobre rubber glove.</li>
          <li><strong>Nitrile:</strong> Para refrigerant (R-410A causes frostbite \u2014 use A2+ rated insulated), acid wash.</li>
          <li><strong>Cold protection:</strong> Insulated A4 gloves para walk-in freezer service.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Fall protection \u2014 8 CCR 1670 (6 ft trigger)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Trigger height:</strong> 6 ft en California (residential construction). Trabajo sobre 6 ft requires fall protection system.</li>
          <li><strong>Full-body harness:</strong> ANSI Z359.11, rated 5,000 lb D-ring, date-stamped inside label. Inspect pre-use for cuts, burns, UV damage.</li>
          <li><strong>Self-Retracting Lifeline (SRL):</strong> ANSI Z359.14 Class B (preferred para roof work, 2 ft freefall limit).</li>
          <li><strong>Anchor point:</strong> 5,000 lb rated o 2x maximum arresting force (2:1 safety factor). Rooftop ridge straps son 2,000 lb = insuficiente sin engineered engineering approval.</li>
          <li><strong>Rescue plan:</strong> 8 CCR 3210 obliga un rescue plan documented. Un tech suspendido en harness puede morir de suspension trauma en 15-30 min. Rescue debe ser 5 min max.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Respiratory protection \u2014 R-410A OEL</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulaci\xF3n:</strong> 8 CCR 5144</li>
          <li><strong>R-410A OEL:</strong> No Cal/OSHA PEL pero AIHA recomienda 1,000 ppm WEEL-TWA. En confined space (attic, mechanical room), ventilation required.</li>
          <li><strong>R-22:</strong> Cal/OSHA PEL 1,000 ppm TWA</li>
          <li><strong>Ammonia (NH3):</strong> 25 ppm TWA / 35 ppm STEL (commercial refrigeration)</li>
          <li><strong>N95 dust mask:</strong> Para insulation work (fiberglass), NOT para refrigerants</li>
          <li><strong>Half-face respirator:</strong> OV (organic vapor) cartridge para acid wash fumes, needs fit-test (medical clearance obligatorio under 5144)</li>
          <li><strong>SCBA:</strong> Para ammonia leak response, confined space > IDLH (Immediately Dangerous to Life or Health) concentration</li>
          <li><strong>Written respiratory program:</strong> Required if any respirator used \u2014 fit test annual, medical clearance, training (5144(f))</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Dielectric / EH-rated footwear</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Standard:</strong> ASTM F2413-18 "EH" (Electrical Hazard) \u2014 18,000V dry resistance</li>
          <li><strong>Safety toe:</strong> Composite or steel toe, 75 ft-lb impact</li>
          <li><strong>Slip resistance:</strong> SRC rating for wet/oily surfaces (roof membranes, leaked refrigerant oil)</li>
          <li><strong>NO metatarsal guard NO</strong> \u2014 metal foot guard is conductive and voids EH rating</li>
          <li><strong>Replace every 6-12 months</strong> \u2014 EH degrades with wear, moisture, sole damage</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>High-visibility clothing \u2014 ANSI/ISEA 107</h4>
        <p>Class 2 o Class 3 vest required si trabajas en right-of-way, parking lots con traffic, o en commercial con forklift traffic. Class 2 para daylight; Class 3 para nighttime or highway.</p>

        <h4>Hearing protection \u2014 85 dBA action level</h4>
        <p>8 CCR 5097-5100. Cuando noise exceeds 85 dBA TWA, empleador debe implementar hearing conservation program (audiometric testing annual, hearing protection available). Duct blower testing = 95-105 dBA. Compressor rooms = 90-95 dBA. Use foam plugs (NRR 29) o earmuffs (NRR 25).</p>
      `,keyPoints:["Hard hat Class E (electrical) \u2014 no Class G o C para HVAC",'Safety glasses ANSI Z87+ con side shields \u2014 "Z87" solo no basta',"Gloves A4+ para sheet metal; dielectric Class 0 para residential","Fall protection en 6 ft (CA stricter que federal)","Respirator requires fit test + medical clearance (8 CCR 5144)","Dielectric boots ASTM F2413 EH \u2014 NO metatarsal guard","Hearing protection cuando exceeds 85 dBA (compressor rooms, duct test)"],realTalk:'"Compr\xE9 $2,000 de hard hats Class G en Home Depot y Cal/OSHA me cit\xF3 porque no eran Class E. La citation fue $8,400 \u2014 m\xE1s caro que comprar los Class E desde el d\xEDa uno. Peor: un tech se electrocut\xF3 levemente con un disconnect y la attorney me pregunt\xF3 por rating de hard hats. Class G no detiene un arc flash. Compra Class E siempre."',checklist:["Hard hat Class E con fecha de manufacture < 5 a\xF1os","Safety glasses Z87+ con side shields","Guantes A4+ + dielectric Class 0 + leather protector","Harness Z359.11 + SRL Z359.14 Class B","Dielectric boots ASTM F2413 EH","Respirator con fit test annual si aplica","Hi-vis vest Class 2+ para traffic areas","Earmuffs/plugs NRR 25+ para noise areas"],commonMistakes:["Comprar hard hats Class G \u2014 no suficiente para electrical",'Dress glasses Z87 sin "+" \u2014 no impact rated',"Harness sin fecha visible \u2014 Cal/OSHA asume expired","Metatarsal guard sobre EH boot \u2014 anula rating","Respirator sin fit test \u2014 citation $5K autom\xE1tica","N95 para refrigerant \u2014 no protege contra gases"]},{id:"lockout-tagout",heading:"LOTO \u2014 Lockout/Tagout (8 CCR 3314)",body:`
        <p><strong>LOTO mata m\xE1s HVAC techs que cualquier otra cosa.</strong> M\xE1s que falls, m\xE1s que heat. Porque cada tech HVAC piensa que sabe electrical \u2014 "yo ya apagu\xE9 el breaker, es seguro" \u2014 hasta el d\xEDa que un capacitor retained 370VAC le pega el pecho o un co-worker turns on the disconnect while you're working on the contactor.</p>

        <p>Lockout/Tagout bajo <strong>8 CCR 3314 "Control of Hazardous Energy"</strong> es el procedure m\xE1s importante que tu company debe dominar. Se aplica a <strong>cualquier</strong> trabajo en equipment donde unexpected energization podr\xEDa lesionar \u2014 eso es 90% del work HVAC (service calls, repairs, installs, PMs).</p>

        <h4>Los 8 pasos del LOTO obligatorios</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Preparation:</strong> Identifica todas las energy sources \u2014 electrical (primary AND secondary, como 24V control), pneumatic, hydraulic, thermal (steam, hot water), mechanical (springs, gravity), chemical (refrigerant pressure), stored energy (capacitors, compressed gas).</li>
          <li><strong>Shutdown:</strong> Apaga el equipment usando el procedure normal (not just flipping a breaker \u2014 follow manufacturer sequence).</li>
          <li><strong>Isolation:</strong> A\xEDsla cada energy source \u2014 abre disconnects, cierra gas valves, bleed refrigerant pressure, disconnect batteries.</li>
          <li><strong>Lockout:</strong> Aplica lock f\xEDsico al disconnect/valve/control. Cada tech trabajando pone SU propio lock (group lockout box si multiple techs).</li>
          <li><strong>Tagout:</strong> Etiqueta con tu nombre, fecha, contacto. Tag no reemplaza lock \u2014 es suplementario. "DO NOT OPERATE" en rojo.</li>
          <li><strong>Stored energy release:</strong> CR\xCDTICO en HVAC \u2014 <strong>discharge capacitors</strong> (resistor 20k\u03A9 5W entre terminals por 30 seg MINIMUM, o use CAT-rated discharge tool). Bleed refrigerant pressure to recovery. Release spring tension. Drain hydraulic accumulators.</li>
          <li><strong>Verification:</strong> Intenta encender el equipment (press start, turn switch) para confirmar que NO arranca. Usa voltmeter para verificar que no hay voltage residual EN CADA PHASE to ground. Meter debe ser CAT III/IV 1000V m\xEDnimo y probado en known source antes Y despu\xE9s (live-dead-live test).</li>
          <li><strong>Work, then re-energize:</strong> Haz el trabajo. Cuando termines, remove tools, remove all personnel from hazard zone, remove your own lock (NEVER remove another tech's lock), re-energize siguiendo el procedure.</li>
        </ol>

        <h4>Por qu\xE9 HVAC guys mueren de esto</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Capacitor retention:</strong> Un capacitor de 45 \u03BCF en un 5-ton AC retains 370VAC por HORAS despu\xE9s de power off si no tiene bleed resistor funcional. Tocar los terminals = parada card\xEDaca.</li>
          <li><strong>Backfeed:</strong> Heat pump en emergency heat mode, generator backup, solar inverter \u2014 todos pueden backfeed al equipment aunque el main disconnect est\xE1 off.</li>
          <li><strong>Control circuit 24V:</strong> Thermostat wiring parece inofensivo pero puede tener 24VAC stepping up to high voltage a trav\xE9s de transformer shorting.</li>
          <li><strong>Someone else turns it on:</strong> Sin lock f\xEDsico, el homeowner, otro tech, o una timer turn on el equipment mientras tu mano est\xE1 en el contactor.</li>
          <li><strong>Line voltage en low-voltage side:</strong> Burned transformer can put 120V/240V en el 24V circuit \u2014 kills if you grab the thermostat wires with no LOTO verification.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Machine-Specific LOTO Forms</h4>
        <p>8 CCR 3314(g) requires <strong>written LOTO procedure for each specific type of equipment</strong> que tu company services. No es un gen\xE9rico para todo. Debes tener procedures documentados para:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Residential split system (condenser + furnace/air handler)</li>
          <li>Rooftop package unit</li>
          <li>Mini-split ductless (outdoor unit + indoor heads)</li>
          <li>Commercial RTU con variable frequency drive (VFD)</li>
          <li>Chiller (water-cooled or air-cooled)</li>
          <li>Boiler (gas or oil)</li>
          <li>Walk-in refrigeration</li>
          <li>VRF/VRV systems (multi-zone)</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Cada procedure en 1 p\xE1gina con: equipment name, energy sources, shutdown sequence, isolation points, stored energy (capacitors, refrigerant pressure, pneumatic), verification method, return-to-service steps.</p>

        <h4>Training requirement \u2014 Annual</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Authorized employee training:</strong> Cada tech que hace LOTO needs documented training \u2014 initial 4-8 hrs + annual refresher 1-2 hrs (8 CCR 3314(k))</li>
          <li><strong>Affected employee training:</strong> Techs que trabajan cerca de locked-out equipment pero no lo operan \u2014 1 hr annual</li>
          <li><strong>Other employee:</strong> Helpers/apprentices \u2014 awareness training 30 min</li>
          <li><strong>Records retention:</strong> 3 a\xF1os m\xEDnimo; recommended 5 a\xF1os</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Electrical LOTO espec\xEDfico \u2014 NFPA 70E</h4>
        <p>Para work en energized equipment (troubleshooting con meter \u2014 unavoidable en HVAC diagnostics), sigue <strong>NFPA 70E</strong>:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Establish Electrically Safe Work Condition (ESWC) = full LOTO cuando posible</li>
          <li>Si tienes que work energized (diagnostic): arc flash PPE category seg\xFAn incident energy (cal/cm\xB2) \u2014 para residential 240V generally Category 1-2, commercial 480V Category 2-4</li>
          <li>Arc-rated shirt/pants (ATPV \u2265 8 cal/cm\xB2 HRC 2), face shield with hood, arc-rated gloves</li>
          <li>Minimum Approach Boundary (MAB) \u2014 Limited, Restricted, Prohibited seg\xFAn NFPA 70E Table 130.4</li>
        </ul>
      `,keyPoints:["LOTO aplica a 90% del trabajo HVAC \u2014 no es opcional","Descarga capacitors con resistor 20k\u03A9 5W o CAT-rated tool","Live-Dead-Live test con meter CAT III/IV 1000V","NO remueves lock de otro tech \u2014 nunca, bajo ninguna circunstancia","Procedure escrito por cada tipo de equipment (3314(g))","Training annual documented para authorized employees","Energized work requires NFPA 70E arc flash PPE"],realTalk:'"Lorenzo ten\xEDa 14 a\xF1os de experiencia. Un martes en Riverside, 5-ton residential service call, flipped the disconnect, fue a cambiar un run capacitor. El capacitor hab\xEDa retained voltage porque el bleed resistor estaba open. Agarr\xF3 los terminals con una mano, la otra toc\xF3 el cabinet. El current pas\xF3 thru el pecho. Su hijo de 3 a\xF1os se qued\xF3 sin pap\xE1. Ten\xEDa LOTO training en Texas, no en California. No descarg\xF3 el capacitor. 30 segundos de resistor y segu\xEDa vivo."',checklist:["Lock + tag personal para cada tech","Resistor 20k\u03A9 5W o CAT discharge tool en cada truck","Multimeter Fluke CAT III/IV 1000V calibrado","Machine-specific procedures escritos para cada equipment type","Training records annual por cada authorized employee","Group lockout box para multi-tech jobs","Arc flash PPE para energized work","LOTO topic rotado en toolbox talks cada 3-4 meses"],commonMistakes:["Solo apagar breaker sin lock \u2014 alguien lo prende","No descargar capacitors \u2014 mata m\xE1s que high voltage","Meter barato sin CAT rating \u2014 arc flash cuando testeas 480V","Procedure gen\xE9rico en vez de machine-specific",'Remover lock de otro tech para "terminar el trabajo"',"Diagnostic energized sin arc flash PPE","Olvidar secondary energy (gas valve, condensate pump, etc.)"]},{id:"heat-illness",heading:"Prevenci\xF3n de Heat Illness (8 CCR 3395)",body:`
        <p>California tiene el <strong>heat illness prevention standard m\xE1s estricto del pa\xEDs</strong>. Naci\xF3 en 2005 despu\xE9s de muertes en Central Valley agriculture, expandi\xF3 a outdoor construction en 2010, y desde <strong>2024 aplica tambi\xE9n a indoor work</strong> (attics, mechanical rooms, warehouses). HVAC techs est\xE1n en el crosshair porque:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Trabajan en roofs en verano 100\xB0F+ con membrane reflejando calor</li>
          <li>Entran a \xE1ticos con ambient 130-160\xB0F</li>
          <li>Cargan equipment pesado en direct sun</li>
          <li>Usan PPE (hard hat, gloves) que blocks evaporative cooling</li>
          <li>Muchos son reci\xE9n llegados (no acclimatized) de climas templados</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Outdoor trigger: 80\xB0F (ambient) \u2014 8 CCR 3395</h4>
        <p>Cuando temp outdoor hits 80\xB0F, ALL estos requirements kick in:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Water:</strong> 1 quart (32 oz) per hour per employee, cool, potable, en location accesible. Cada tech acceso a agua a < 400 ft o 5 min walk. En hot days ofrecer electrolyte drinks.</li>
          <li><strong>Shade:</strong> Open-sided, blocks direct sun (tarp, tent, truck bed con umbrella), capacity para todos on rest break simult\xE1neamente. Must be present at ALL times when temp > 80\xB0F (not "available when requested").</li>
          <li><strong>Rest breaks:</strong> Allowed and encouraged for 5+ min cuando employee feels need. Paid breaks.</li>
          <li><strong>Training:</strong> Heat illness symptoms (headache, dizziness, nausea, confusion, cessation of sweating), what to do if sick, how to report.</li>
          <li><strong>Emergency response procedures:</strong> How to call 911, exact address of job site, clear path for ambulance.</li>
          <li><strong>Acclimatization:</strong> New employees closely observed for first 14 days. Take it easy \u2014 20% reduction Day 1, scaling up.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>High-Heat procedures: 95\xB0F trigger</h4>
        <p>Cuando temp hits 95\xB0F, additional requirements:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Effective observation:</strong> Foreman observes crew \u2014 no more than 20 workers per supervisor OR implement mandatory buddy system</li>
          <li><strong>Pre-shift meetings:</strong> Every shift reviewing heat illness prevention</li>
          <li><strong>Mandatory preventative cool-down rest:</strong> Minimum 10 min every 2 hrs of work, paid</li>
          <li><strong>Communication:</strong> Electronic device (radio, phone) to summon emergency services from anywhere</li>
          <li><strong>Frequent water reminders:</strong> Every 15 min when possible</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Indoor trigger \u2014 8 CCR 3396 (effective 2024)</h4>
        <p>El nuevo standard indoor (effective July 23, 2024) aplica cuando temp indoor \u2265 82\xB0F O heat index \u2265 87\xB0F:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Cool-down areas:</strong> Space with temp < 82\xB0F accessible para rest</li>
          <li><strong>Water, shade/cool area, rest, acclimatization</strong> \u2014 same as outdoor</li>
          <li><strong>Engineering controls:</strong> Fans, ventilation, spot coolers, air conditioning cuando feasible</li>
          <li><strong>82\xB0F indoor + PPE/radiant heat sources:</strong> Additional assessment required (attics, mechanical rooms con boilers, welding)</li>
          <li><strong>Measurement:</strong> Use wet bulb globe temperature (WBGT) for radiant environments o heat index from dry bulb + humidity</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Aplica especialmente a \xE1ticos y rooftops</strong> \u2014 donde la mayor\xEDa de HVAC techs trabajan. Un attic en Sacramento en Julio puede estar a 145\xB0F \u2014 requires cool-down rotation cada 15-30 min max.</p>

        <h4>Acclimatization protocol (14 days)</h4>
        <p>El 80% de muertes por heat illness son employees con < 14 d\xEDas de experiencia en calor de esa intensidad. Protocol:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Day 1:</strong> 20% of normal work exposure max</li>
          <li><strong>Day 2:</strong> 40%</li>
          <li><strong>Day 3:</strong> 60%</li>
          <li><strong>Day 4:</strong> 80%</li>
          <li><strong>Day 5-14:</strong> Gradual to 100%, close monitoring</li>
          <li>Apply for ANY new employee o employees returning despu\xE9s de 1 week ausencia (vacation, illness)</li>
          <li>Buddy system \u2014 no trabajar solo en heat first 14 days</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Emergency Action Plan (EAP) para heat illness</h4>
        <p>Si tech shows heat stroke signs (confusion, no sweating, skin hot/dry/red, body temp > 104\xB0F):</p>
        <ol>
          <li>Call 911 INMEDIATAMENTE (no esperar para "ver si mejora")</li>
          <li>Move to cool/shaded area</li>
          <li>Loosen clothing, remove PPE</li>
          <li>Active cooling: ice packs on neck, armpits, groin; spray with water + fan; immersion in cool water if available</li>
          <li>NO dar nada de beber si est\xE1 confused/unconscious (aspiration risk)</li>
          <li>Monitor breathing, prepare for CPR</li>
          <li>Give EMS exact address + "probable heat stroke, core temp unknown, onset ___ min ago"</li>
        </ol>
        <p><strong>Heat stroke medical emergency \u2014 minutes matter.</strong> Una persona puede morir en 30 min si core temp stays > 105\xB0F. Employers who delay calling 911 face criminal negligence charges.</p>

        <h4>Heat illness written procedures</h4>
        <p>Required por 3395(i) \u2014 tu IIPP debe tener secc\xF3n espec\xEDfica de heat covering: water access, shade access, cool-down rest breaks, emergency response procedures, acclimatization, training, high-heat procedures. Must be en idioma que employees understand. Available at job site.</p>
      `,keyPoints:["Outdoor trigger 80\xB0F, high-heat trigger 95\xB0F, indoor trigger 82\xB0F/87\xB0F","Water 1 qt/hr/person + shade + 5-min rest breaks on demand","Acclimatization 14 d\xEDas para new hires \u2014 80% de muertes son newbies","High-heat 95\xB0F: mandatory 10 min rest every 2 hrs","Indoor standard (2024) aplica a \xE1ticos y mechanical rooms","Heat stroke = call 911, active cooling, no beber","Written procedures en idioma de employees (Espa\xF1ol)"],realTalk:'"El standard de heat illness es el m\xE1s enforced por Cal/OSHA en verano \u2014 tienen equipos dedicados a random inspections en junio-septiembre. Si no tienes shade, water, rest protocol, y EAP escrito \u2014 citation Serious $25K inmediato. Y Dios no quiera que un tech muera \u2014 he visto companies perder el CSLB license por involuntary manslaughter (Penal 192(b)) cuando owner ignored a tech complaining of symptoms."',checklist:["Agua fresca 1 qt/hr/person en cooler con ice","Shade structure (tarp + truck, pop-up tent) en cada job site","Electrolyte packets disponibles (Gatorade, LMNT)","Thermometer para verificar ambient temp","Acclimatization schedule documentado para new hires","Buddy system assignments durante heat","EAP con exact address + 911 script","Heat illness training annual + toolbox refresher en spring","Written procedures 3395(i) en espa\xF1ol"],commonMistakes:['Solo tener agua "si la piden" \u2014 shade/water must be present PROACTIVELY',"Ignorar indoor standard en \xE1ticos \u2014 new 2024 rule que muchos no conocen","Poner new hire en roof d\xEDa 1 \u2014 acclimatization protocol violado","No llamar 911 inmediatamente cuando ven confusion \u2014 crimen","Dar agua a alguien con heat stroke unconscious \u2014 aspiration","Training solo en ingl\xE9s cuando crew es hispano \u2014 citation","No documentar training \u2014 legalmente no existi\xF3"]},{id:"incident-reporting",heading:"Incident Reporting \u2014 300/300A/301 y Serious Injury",body:`
        <p>Cuando hay un incident, tienes m\xE1ximo <strong>8 horas</strong> para reportar casos serios a Cal/OSHA. Fallar esto es automatic Serious citation + evidence tampering allegations si paralelamente arreglas el hazard. Este secci\xF3n cubre todos los reporting requirements: OSHA logs, Workers Comp, y Cal/OSHA serious injury reporting.</p>

        <h4>Cal/OSHA 300 Log \u2014 Recordable Injuries</h4>
        <p>Required para TODO employer con 11+ empleados (8 CCR 14300). HVAC companies con menos de 11 est\xE1n exempt del log pero NO exempt de serious injury reporting. Cases que DEBES log en Form 300:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Death</strong> \u2014 cualquier muerte en el job</li>
          <li><strong>Days away from work</strong> \u2014 employee no puede trabajar al menos 1 d\xEDa despu\xE9s del incident</li>
          <li><strong>Restricted work / job transfer</strong> \u2014 limited duty, light duty assignment</li>
          <li><strong>Medical treatment beyond first aid</strong> \u2014 prescription meds, stitches, x-rays, IV fluids (first aid = OTC meds, butterfly bandages, heat/cold packs \u2014 does NOT count as recordable)</li>
          <li><strong>Loss of consciousness</strong> \u2014 aunque sea 30 segundos</li>
          <li><strong>Significant injury diagnosed by PLHCP</strong> (physician or licensed healthcare professional) \u2014 fractured/broken bones, punctured eardrum, cancer, chronic irreversible disease</li>
          <li><strong>Needlestick/sharps injury</strong> \u2014 any</li>
          <li><strong>Medical removal</strong> \u2014 per Cal/OSHA standard (e.g., lead exposure)</li>
          <li><strong>Hearing loss</strong> \u2014 Standard Threshold Shift of 10 dB average plus hearing level 25 dB</li>
          <li><strong>Tuberculosis</strong> \u2014 if occupationally acquired</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Form 300A \u2014 Annual Summary</h4>
        <p>Summary of total cases por a\xF1o calendar. <strong>Posted February 1 through April 30</strong> cada a\xF1o en location visible to employees. Signed by company executive. Even if zero cases, still post "0" for the year.</p>

        <h4>Form 301 \u2014 Injury and Illness Incident Report</h4>
        <p>Detailed narrative para cada recordable case. Keep on file 5 a\xF1os. Privacy-sensitive cases (HIV, sexual assault, etc.) can use alternate form con redacted name.</p>

        <h4>Electronic submission \u2014 Cal/OSHA ITA</h4>
        <p>Since 2024, employers con 100+ empleados en high-hazard industries (HVAC is included) must submit 300/300A/301 electronically to Cal/OSHA Injury Tracking Application by March 2 annually. Smaller companies submit 300A only.</p>

        <h4>SERIOUS INJURY REPORT \u2014 8 hour clock</h4>
        <p>Este es el biggie. Under <strong>8 CCR 342</strong>, report to Cal/OSHA <strong>within 8 hours</strong> (by phone + written follow-up) cualquier:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Death</strong> of any employee on the job</li>
          <li><strong>Serious injury or illness</strong> \u2014 defined as:
            <ul>
              <li>Inpatient hospitalization for other than medical observation (overnight admission, surgical procedure)</li>
              <li>Amputation (loss of body part \u2014 even partial finger)</li>
              <li>Loss of an eye (any permanent eye damage)</li>
              <li>Serious degree of permanent disfigurement</li>
            </ul>
          </li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>C\xF3mo reportar:</strong></p>
        <ul>
          <li>Call nearest Cal/OSHA district office (listed at dir.ca.gov/dosh) \u2014 have the phone numbers saved in cada tech's phone</li>
          <li>Follow up with written report online at dir.ca.gov or by mail within 24 hrs</li>
          <li>Preserve the accident scene \u2014 do NOT clean up, do NOT repair equipment, do NOT interview witnesses until Cal/OSHA arrives (they want the scene intact)</li>
          <li>Only exception: you can alter scene to render it safe if there's continuing hazard (e.g., active electrical hazard). Document thoroughly with photos before.</li>
        </ul>
        <p><strong>Miss the 8-hour clock = $5,000 - $25,000 penalty</strong> automatic, plus inspection trigger that often finds otras violations.</p>

        <h4>Workers' Comp First Report of Injury (FROI)</h4>
        <p>Separate from Cal/OSHA. Required within <strong>5 days</strong> of knowledge of injury, but best practice = immediately:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>DWC-1 Claim Form</strong> \u2014 give to injured employee within 1 working day, keep copy signed</li>
          <li><strong>Form 5020</strong> (Employer's Report of Occupational Injury or Illness) \u2014 submit to workers comp carrier within 5 days</li>
          <li><strong>DWC-7 Notice</strong> \u2014 post in workplace showing carrier info, claims adjuster, medical provider network (MPN)</li>
          <li><strong>Benefits:</strong> Medical treatment 100% covered, 2/3 of wages (TTD) starting day 4 (or day 1 if hospitalized or off > 14 days), permanent disability if applicable</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>MPN \u2014 Medical Provider Network</h4>
        <p>Most WC carriers have MPN \u2014 injured employee must see network docs for first 30 days. After that, employee can change. Pre-designation: before injury, employee can designate their own doctor (limited circumstances). Post DWC-9 in workplace.</p>

        <h4>Return-to-work y Light Duty</h4>
        <p>Offer modified/light duty when possible. Reduces lost-time claims (which drive up Experience Mod X-Mod). X-Mod 1.25 = 25% more premium; 2.0 = unable to get coverage. Light duty con restrictions from doc (e.g., "no lifting > 10 lbs, no climbing ladders, desk work only") is preferred over employee sitting at home for weeks.</p>

        <h4>Retaliation prohibited \u2014 Labor Code 132a</h4>
        <p>You CANNOT fire, demote, or discriminate against employee who files Workers Comp claim. Doing so = 132a penalty up to $10,000 + reinstatement + back wages + 1/2 increased comp. Documentation of legitimate non-retaliation reason for any action is critical.</p>
      `,keyPoints:["Serious injury = 8 hours to report Cal/OSHA \u2014 death, hospitalization, amputation, eye loss","Preserve scene untouched hasta que llegue Cal/OSHA","OSHA 300 log para todo recordable \u2014 5 a\xF1os retention","300A posted Feb 1 - Apr 30 cada a\xF1o","DWC-1 to employee dentro 1 working day; Form 5020 within 5 days","Retaliation = Labor Code 132a, hasta $10K penalty","Light duty reduces X-Mod, lower WC premiums"],realTalk:'"Un tech se cay\xF3 de un ladder \u2014 roto el h\xFAmero, hospitalizado overnight. El owner pens\xF3 \u2018no es tan grave, lo arreglamos ma\xF1ana.\u2019 Pero \u2018inpatient overnight\u2019 = serious injury = 8-hour report a Cal/OSHA. Cuando Cal/OSHA lleg\xF3 3 d\xEDas despu\xE9s v\xEDa complaint de la esposa del tech, citation por failure to report $18,500 + 7 otras citations ($89K total). M\xE1s un plaintiff attorney tom\xF3 el caso y settled por $380K. Todo por no llamar un n\xFAmero en 8 horas."',checklist:["Cal/OSHA district office phone en cada tech phone + truck","OSHA 300 log updated dentro 7 d\xEDas de cada recordable","300A posted Feb 1 - Apr 30 cada a\xF1o","DWC-1 forms pre-printed en cada truck","MPN info + pre-designation form en hiring packet","Written incident investigation procedure","Light duty program escrito para reduce X-Mod","Labor Code 132a anti-retaliation training to supervisors"],commonMistakes:['Demorar serious injury report para "investigar" \u2014 clock corre igual',"Cleanear la scene antes que Cal/OSHA llegue \u2014 evidence tampering","No dar DWC-1 al injured employee \u2014 132a violation","Confundir first aid con medical treatment \u2014 over/under-record","No postear 300A \u2014 citation per d\xEDa que falta","Firing empleado post-claim \u2014 132a + wrongful termination","Sin light duty program \u2014 X-Mod sube, premiums disparan"]},{id:"citations-fines",heading:"Citations y Multas \u2014 C\xF3mo Apelar",body:`
        <p>Cuando Cal/OSHA termina una inspection, puedes recibir citations. Entender los tipos, los rangos de multa, el proceso de apelaci\xF3n, y las top violations que pegan a HVAC te ayuda a prevenir \u2014 o pelear inteligentemente si ya cayeron.</p>

        <h4>Tipos de violations y rangos (2025)</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Regulatory:</strong> Failure to post, failure to maintain records, failure to report. $500 - $15,873 per violation. Por ejemplo, no postear 300A = regulatory.</li>
          <li><strong>General:</strong> Violations que podr\xEDan cause injury pero no est\xE1n categorized como serious. $500 - $15,873 per violation. Ejemplo: housekeeping hazard, poor training documentation.</li>
          <li><strong>Serious:</strong> Substantial probability of death o serious physical harm. Minimum $18,000, max $25,000 per violation. La mayor\xEDa de HVAC violations caen aqu\xED (no fall protection, no LOTO, no IIPP).</li>
          <li><strong>Willful:</strong> Intentional, knowing, or with plain indifference. Minimum $11,338 hasta $158,727 per violation. Si sab\xEDas del hazard y no lo arreglaste.</li>
          <li><strong>Repeat:</strong> Substantially similar violation dentro de 5 a\xF1os. Penalty tripled, hasta $158,727. Repeat fall protection citation = empresa muerta.</li>
          <li><strong>Failure to Abate:</strong> No correction dentro del deadline. $15,873 per day past abatement date.</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>2025 maximum fine examples</h4>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>No IIPP (Serious):</strong> $25,000 per violation</li>
          <li><strong>No fall protection at 6 ft (Serious):</strong> $25,000</li>
          <li><strong>No fall protection + prior citation (Repeat):</strong> $75,000+</li>
          <li><strong>No LOTO resulting in death (Willful):</strong> $158,727 per violation (and cases often have multiple)</li>
          <li><strong>Failure to report serious injury:</strong> $5,000 - $25,000</li>
          <li><strong>Inadequate heat illness program in 95\xB0F weather:</strong> $25,000 Serious</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Top 10 Cal/OSHA citations para HVAC (Construction Safety Orders)</h4>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Fall protection</strong> (8 CCR 1670) \u2014 missing guardrails, no PFAS, improper anchor</li>
          <li><strong>Ladder misuse</strong> (8 CCR 3276) \u2014 extension not 3 ft above landing, angle wrong, not secured</li>
          <li><strong>Hazard Communication / GHS labeling</strong> (8 CCR 5194) \u2014 no SDS binder, improper labels, untrained employees</li>
          <li><strong>Respiratory protection</strong> (8 CCR 5144) \u2014 no fit test, no medical clearance, no written program</li>
          <li><strong>Fall protection training</strong> (8 CCR 1671) \u2014 no documented training before work at heights</li>
          <li><strong>Scaffolding</strong> (8 CCR 1637) \u2014 improperly erected, no competent person, no guardrails</li>
          <li><strong>Electrical safety</strong> (8 CCR 2299 + NFPA 70E) \u2014 no LOTO, improper GFCI, missing covers</li>
          <li><strong>Personal Protective Equipment</strong> (8 CCR 3380) \u2014 wrong rating, not provided, not used</li>
          <li><strong>IIPP deficiencies</strong> (8 CCR 3203) \u2014 not in writing, no training docs, no inspections</li>
          <li><strong>Heat Illness Prevention</strong> (8 CCR 3395/3396) \u2014 no water/shade/rest, no acclimatization, no EAP</li>
        </ol>

        <h4>Appeal process \u2014 7 Working Days</h4>
        <p>Cuando recibes citations, tienes <strong>15 working days</strong> para appeal (not 7 \u2014 el 7 es el informal conference request). Los pasos:</p>
        <ol>
          <li><strong>Day 0:</strong> Receive citation in mail or hand-delivered</li>
          <li><strong>Within 15 working days:</strong> File Notice of Appeal con OSHSAB (Occupational Safety and Health Appeals Board). Extend by 10 days if you request informal conference.</li>
          <li><strong>Informal Conference (optional, within 7 working days):</strong> Meet with Cal/OSHA district manager. Often reduces penalties 30-50% if you show good faith, abatement, history.</li>
          <li><strong>Formal Appeal Hearing:</strong> Before Administrative Law Judge (ALJ). Can represent yourself pero attorney recommended for Serious/Willful cases. Employer burden to prove Cal/OSHA wrong.</li>
          <li><strong>Decision after Hearing:</strong> Upheld, modified, or vacated</li>
          <li><strong>Reconsideration (within 30 days):</strong> If unhappy, request review by the OSHSAB board</li>
          <li><strong>Superior Court review:</strong> Writ of mandate, only on narrow legal grounds</li>
        </ol>

        <h4>Negotiating penalty reduction</h4>
        <p>Most successful appeals result in penalty reduction, not dismissal. Arguments que funcionan:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Good faith:</strong> Pre-existing IIPP, training records, safety committee</li>
          <li><strong>Size of employer:</strong> Small (< 10 employees) gets 80% reduction; medium (11-100) 60%; large (101-250) 40%</li>
          <li><strong>History:</strong> No prior citations past 3 years = 10% reduction</li>
          <li><strong>Abatement:</strong> Immediate correction with documentation</li>
          <li><strong>Employee misconduct defense:</strong> If tech violated your written rule you enforced, reduce to General or vacate</li>
          <li><strong>Economic feasibility:</strong> Some standards allow showing that compliance is economically infeasible</li>
          <li><strong>Classification reduction:</strong> Willful \u2192 Serious \u2192 General \u2192 Regulatory, each step drops fine massively</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Criminal liability \u2014 Penal Code 192(b) & Labor Code 6425</h4>
        <p>When an employee dies due to willful violation, Cal/OSHA can refer for criminal prosecution:</p>
        <ul>
          <li><strong>Labor Code 6425:</strong> Willful violation causing death = misdemeanor for employer \u2014 up to $250,000 individual / $1.5M corporation, up to 1 year jail</li>
          <li><strong>Penal Code 192(b) Involuntary Manslaughter:</strong> If gross negligence contributed to death \u2014 felony, up to 4 years prison</li>
          <li><strong>Corporate officer personal liability:</strong> Owner/officer can be individually charged if they knew of hazard and didn't correct</li>
        </ul>
        <p>Cal/OSHA has referred 200+ cases to DA's office in past decade. Most egregious: a Los Angeles roofing contractor got 2 years prison for worker death after 3 prior fall protection citations.</p>

        <h4>Impact en CSLB license</h4>
        <p>Cal/OSHA does not directly suspend CSLB license, BUT:</p>
        <ul>
          <li>Willful violation + fatality = automatic referral to CSLB for Board review</li>
          <li>Unpaid penalties \u2192 tax lien \u2192 affects licensing renewal</li>
          <li>Felony conviction = CSLB suspension/revocation mandatory</li>
          <li>Workers Comp coverage lapse (because X-Mod too high to get insured) = immediate CSLB suspension</li>
        </ul>

        <h4>Insurance impact</h4>
        <p>Single Serious citation = GL premium increase 10-25%. Willful = non-renewal probable. WC X-Mod jumps after claims, pricing you out of market. Once uninsured, game over \u2014 can't operate legally.</p>
      `,keyPoints:["Serious violation = $18K-$25K; Willful/Repeat = hasta $158,727 per violation","Multiple violations common \u2014 single inspection can total $100K+","Appeal 15 working days; Informal conference within 7 reduces 30-50%","Employer misconduct defense works if rules written + enforced","Willful + death = Labor Code 6425 misdemeanor; gross negligence = Penal 192(b) felony","CSLB suspension likely after fatal citation","Insurance non-renewal after Willful \u2014 end of company"],realTalk:'"Luis hab\xEDa sido citado 2 veces por fall protection. La tercera vez un tech se cay\xF3 y muri\xF3. Cal/OSHA clasific\xF3 Willful + Repeat = $476,181 en citations. Penal Code 192(b) referral \u2014 pled down to 180 days jail + 5 years probation. CSLB license revoked. Family of tech won wrongful death $3.2M. He perdi\xF3 todo. Un $40 harness hubiera prevented TODO. Safety no es opcional \u2014 es la \xFAnica cosa en tu empresa que si fallas una vez, te destruye."',checklist:["Cada citation receive \u2192 consultar attorney antes de pagar","File informal conference request dentro 7 working days","Documentar abatement con fotos + fechas","Preparar defense: training records, IIPP, toolbox rosters","Employee misconduct defense requires written rule + enforcement history","Keep clean 3-year record (compliance history) para discount","Pagar citations prontamente o arrange payment plan","Never ignore citations \u2014 failure to pay = liens \u2192 license impact"],commonMistakes:["Pagar citation sin appeal \u2014 perdiste 30-50% reduction autom\xE1tica","Missing 15-working-day appeal window \u2014 final, no recurso","Cleaning up scene antes de inspection \u2014 obstruction","Admit fault en informal conference \u2014 used contra ti in trial","No attorney para Serious/Willful \u2014 technical defenses complejos","Ignorar CSLB notification despu\xE9s de fatal citation \u2014 p\xE9rdida license","Cancelar WC despu\xE9s de claim \u2014 triple peligro, CSLB + Cal/OSHA + plaintiff"]}],resources:[{label:"Cal/OSHA Main Site (DIR)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR Title 8 California Code of Regulations",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/samples/search/query.htm",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 3203 \u2014 Injury and Illness Prevention Program (IIPP)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3203.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 3314 \u2014 Control of Hazardous Energy (LOTO)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3314.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 3395 \u2014 Heat Illness Prevention (Outdoor)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3395.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 3396 \u2014 Heat Illness Prevention (Indoor, 2024)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/3396.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 1670 \u2014 Fall Protection (Construction)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/1670.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 1514 \u2014 Head Protection",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/1514.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 5144 \u2014 Respiratory Protection",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/5144.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 5194 \u2014 Hazard Communication (GHS)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/5194.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 14300 \u2014 Recording Workplace Injuries (300/300A/301)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/14300.html",type:"link"},{label:"8 CCR 342 \u2014 Reporting Serious Injury",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/title8/342.html",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA IIPP eTool (free template)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/etools/09-031/index.htm",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA Heat Illness Prevention eTool",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/etools/08-006/index.htm",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA Publications (free booklets)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/dosh_publications/",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA Appeals Board (OSHSAB)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/oshab/",type:"link"},{label:"DWC \u2014 Division of Workers Compensation",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dwc/",type:"link"},{label:"DWC-1 Claim Form",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dwc/forms.html",type:"link"},{label:"DIR Contractor Registration (CRS)",url:"https://efiling.dir.ca.gov/PWCR/",type:"link"},{label:"ANSI Z87.1 Eye Protection Standard",url:"https://www.ansi.org/standard/z87-1-american-national-standard-for-occupational-and-educational-personal-eye-and-face-protection-devices",type:"link"},{label:"ANSI Z89.1 Head Protection Standard",url:"https://www.ansi.org/standard/z89-1-american-national-standard-for-industrial-head-protection",type:"link"},{label:"ANSI/ISEA 105 Hand Protection Standard",url:"https://safetyequipment.org/standard/ansi-isea-105-2016/",type:"link"},{label:"ANSI Z359 Fall Protection Standards",url:"https://www.ansi.org/standard/z359",type:"link"},{label:"NFPA 70E Electrical Safety in the Workplace",url:"https://www.nfpa.org/codes-and-standards/all-codes-and-standards/list-of-codes-and-standards/detail?code=70E",type:"link"},{label:"OSHA 300/300A/301 Forms (federal template, compatible with Cal/OSHA)",url:"https://www.osha.gov/recordkeeping/RKforms",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA Injury Tracking Application (ITA) \u2014 Electronic Submission",url:"https://www.osha.gov/injuryreporting/",type:"link"},{label:"NIOSH Pocket Guide to Chemical Hazards",url:"https://www.cdc.gov/niosh/npg/",type:"link"},{label:"Cal/OSHA District Offices (to report serious injuries)",url:"https://www.dir.ca.gov/dosh/districtoffices.htm",type:"link"},{label:"Labor Code 6425 (Willful Violation Causing Death)",url:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=6425.&lawCode=LAB",type:"link"},{label:"Penal Code 192(b) \u2014 Involuntary Manslaughter",url:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=192.&lawCode=PEN",type:"link"},{label:"Labor Code 132a \u2014 Anti-Retaliation",url:"https://leginfo.legislature.ca.gov/faces/codes_displaySection.xhtml?sectionNum=132a.&lawCode=LAB",type:"link"}],glossary:[{term:"IIPP (Injury & Illness Prevention Program)",def:"Programa escrito de seguridad obligatorio para todo employer en CA con 1+ empleado (8 CCR 3203). Debe incluir responsible person, compliance system, communication, hazard assessment, accident investigation, hazard correction, training, recordkeeping."},{term:"JSA (Job Safety Analysis)",def:"Tambi\xE9n conocido como AHA (Activity Hazard Analysis) o Pre-Task Plan. Documento diario que identifica tasks, hazards, controls, y PPE para un job espec\xEDfico antes de empezar. Firmado por cada tech."},{term:"LOTO (Lockout/Tagout)",def:"Procedure bajo 8 CCR 3314 para controlar hazardous energy durante service/maintenance. Incluye isolation, lock f\xEDsico, tag, stored energy release (capacitors), y verification con meter."},{term:"PPE (Personal Protective Equipment)",def:"Equipo de protecci\xF3n personal \u2014 hard hat, safety glasses, gloves, harness, respirator, boots. Cada categoria tiene ANSI standard espec\xEDfico (Z89.1, Z87.1, ISEA 105, Z359, F2413)."},{term:"SDS (Safety Data Sheet)",def:"Hoja de datos de seguridad de productos qu\xEDmicos \u2014 reemplaz\xF3 el MSDS en 2015 bajo GHS. Debe estar disponible para empleados (8 CCR 5194). 16 sections est\xE1ndar."},{term:"GHS (Globally Harmonized System)",def:'Sistema internacional de clasificaci\xF3n y etiquetado de qu\xEDmicos. Pictogramas (flama, calavera, corrosivo, etc.) y signal words ("Danger", "Warning"). Integrado en 8 CCR 5194.'},{term:"ANSI (American National Standards Institute)",def:"Organizaci\xF3n que publica standards voluntarios para PPE (Z87.1 eyes, Z89.1 head, Z359 fall, ISEA 105 hands). Cal/OSHA incorpora estos standards por referencia."},{term:"OEL (Occupational Exposure Limit)",def:"Limite de exposici\xF3n a qu\xEDmicos \u2014 TWA (8-hr average), STEL (15-min short-term), Ceiling (never exceed). R-22 PEL 1,000 ppm TWA. R-410A sin PEL pero AIHA WEEL 1,000 ppm."},{term:"PEL (Permissible Exposure Limit)",def:"Cal/OSHA enforceable exposure limit, usually 8-hour TWA. Found in 8 CCR 5155 (airborne contaminants table)."},{term:"FROI (First Report of Injury)",def:"Form 5020 (Employer's Report of Occupational Injury or Illness) \u2014 submitted to Workers Comp carrier within 5 days de knowledge del injury."},{term:"DWC-1",def:"Workers Compensation Claim Form \u2014 dado al injured employee dentro 1 working day de knowledge del injury. Employer debe keep signed copy."},{term:"MPN (Medical Provider Network)",def:"Red de doctores designados por Workers Comp carrier. Injured employee debe ir con MPN docs primeros 30 d\xEDas, except pre-designated physician."},{term:"X-Mod (Experience Modification Rate)",def:"Factor multiplicador de Workers Comp premium basado en injury history de los \xFAltimos 3 a\xF1os. 1.0 = average. 0.85 = good discount. 1.5+ = severe penalty, 2.0+ = often non-insurable."},{term:"EAP (Emergency Action Plan)",def:"Plan escrito de respuesta a emergencias \u2014 fire, medical, earthquake, chemical spill. Required bajo 8 CCR 3220. Incluye evacuation routes, alarm systems, rescue procedures, accountability."},{term:"EAP vs ERP",def:"EAP = Emergency Action Plan (what to do). ERP = Emergency Response Plan (specific for hazmat, HAZWOPER work). Both may be required depending on materials handled."},{term:"DOSH (Division of Occupational Safety and Health)",def:'La parte de DIR (Department of Industrial Relations) que ejecuta Cal/OSHA. Los inspectors son DOSH compliance officers. "Cal/OSHA" y "DOSH" usados intercambiably.'},{term:"DIR (Department of Industrial Relations)",def:"Agencia estatal que supervisa DOSH, DWC, DLSE, y otros. Responsible del CRS (Contractor Registration System) para prevailing wage jobs."},{term:"OSHSAB (Occupational Safety and Health Appeals Board)",def:"Independent body que hears appeals de Cal/OSHA citations. ALJ (Administrative Law Judge) conducts hearings. Board reviews ALJ decisions."},{term:"Competent Person",def:"Employee designated in writing with training y authority to identify hazards y halt work. Required por scaffold (1637), fall protection (1670), excavation (1540), LOTO (3314)."},{term:"Qualified Person",def:'Employee con specific training, licensing, o credentials para perform task (electrical work, welding, crane operation). Higher bar than "competent person."'},{term:"Recordable Injury",def:"Injury meeting criteria de 8 CCR 14300 \u2014 days away, restricted work, medical treatment beyond first aid, loss of consciousness, significant injury. Logged on OSHA 300."},{term:"First Aid vs Medical Treatment",def:"First aid (OTC, butterfly bandage, heat/cold pack, eye flush) = NOT recordable. Medical treatment (prescription, stitches, x-ray, IV) = recordable. Distinction matters."},{term:"Serious Injury (Cal/OSHA reporting)",def:"Under 8 CCR 342 \u2014 death, inpatient hospitalization (overnight), amputation, loss of eye, serious permanent disfigurement. Trigger 8-hour report requirement."},{term:"Acclimatization",def:"Physiological adaptation a hot environments. Takes 14 d\xEDas para new employee. 80% de muertes por heat son empleados no aclimatados. Protocol: 20% Day 1, scaling up gradually."},{term:"WBGT (Wet Bulb Globe Temperature)",def:"Measurement de heat stress combinando ambient temp, humidity, wind, y radiant heat. Used en 8 CCR 3396 indoor heat standard. Different than just dry-bulb thermometer."},{term:"Willful Violation",def:"Employer knew of hazard y showed plain indifference o intentional disregard. Penalty $11,338 - $158,727. Triggers criminal referral if death involved (Labor Code 6425)."}]};
